# Prisma and data layer – PandaTrack

## Prisma client

The Prisma client is instantiated and exported from `src/lib/prisma.ts`. The project uses the `@prisma/adapter-pg` adapter with `pg` Pool. Do not create another `PrismaClient` instance elsewhere in the app.

## Centralize queries

All Prisma queries must live in dedicated data-layer modules, not scattered in pages, layouts, server actions, components, hooks, or route handlers.

- Primary home: **`src/queries/`**, one file per Prisma model (e.g. `src/queries/store.ts` for the `Store` model, `src/queries/user.ts` for the `User` model).
- Cross-model domain snapshots (e.g. settings, governance) live in the same folder under a domain-named file (e.g. `src/queries/userSettings.ts`, `src/queries/storeGovernance.ts`).
- Organize functions by responsibility (`getXByY`, `listX`, `createX`, `updateX`, `deleteX`, etc.).

### Query function signature

Prefer **dependency injection**: accept the Prisma client (or a transaction client) as the first argument.

```ts
// ✅ Preferred: DI-friendly, easy to test and to run inside a transaction
import type { PrismaClient } from "../../generated/prisma/client";

export async function getStoreBySlug(db: PrismaClient, slug: string) { /* ... */ }
```

Functions that import `prisma` directly are also acceptable when the module is already co-located with other direct-import helpers in that file (keep one convention per file, do not mix).

## Encapsulation

Pages, layouts, server actions, route handlers, React components, hooks, business services, and domain helpers **must not** call Prisma directly. Every `prisma.<model>.<method>(...)`, `db.<model>.<method>(...)`, or `prisma.$transaction(...)` expression must live inside a file under `src/queries/`.

The only places allowed to perform Prisma queries are:

1. **`src/queries/**/*.ts`** — the data layer itself. All CRUD ops, raw SQL, and transactions live here.
2. **Tests** (`**/*.test.ts`, `**/*.integration.test.ts`, `src/test/**`, `**/_tests/**`) — integration tests may use `prisma` directly for setup and assertions.

The only files allowed to import the `prisma` client at all are:

1. **`src/lib/prisma.ts`** — defines and exports the client.
2. **`src/queries/**/*.ts`** — uses the client to run the actual queries.
3. **`src/lib/auth/auth.ts`** — BetterAuth adapter wiring (`prismaAdapter(prisma, ...)`). Library integration only, not ad-hoc queries.
4. Any other file — **only** to pass `prisma` through to a function exported from `src/queries/` (dependency injection). These files must not call `prisma.<model>.<method>(...)` themselves.

This includes `src/lib/<domain>/*.ts` helpers: they may orchestrate domain logic (rate limits, cooldowns, formatting scope ids, building verification payloads, etc.) and then delegate the actual DB work to `src/queries/`. Queries do **not** belong in `src/lib/**`.

### ❌ Bad: inline query in a page

```tsx
// src/app/[locale]/(app)/settings/page.tsx
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma";

export default async function SettingsPage() {
  const user = await prisma.user.findUnique({
    where: { id: session.user.id },
    select: { email: true, username: true, name: true },
  });
  // ...
}
```

### ❌ Bad: inline query in a server action

```ts
// src/app/[locale]/(app)/settings/_actions/profileActions.ts
"use server";
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma";

export async function saveDisplayNameAction(name: string) {
  await prisma.user.update({ where: { id: userId }, data: { name } });
}
```

### ✅ Good: page delegates to a query module

```ts
// src/queries/userSettings.ts
export async function getSettingsPageSnapshot(userId: string) { /* query */ }
```

```tsx
// src/app/[locale]/(app)/settings/page.tsx
import { getSettingsPageSnapshot } from "@/queries/userSettings";

export default async function SettingsPage() {
  const snapshot = await getSettingsPageSnapshot(session.user.id);
  // ...
}
```

### ✅ Good: server action delegates to a query module

```ts
// src/queries/user.ts
import type { PrismaClient } from "../../generated/prisma/client";

export async function updateUserDisplayName(db: PrismaClient, userId: string, name: string) {
  await db.user.update({ where: { id: userId }, data: { name } });
}
```

```ts
// src/app/[locale]/(app)/settings/_actions/profileActions.ts
"use server";
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma";
import { updateUserDisplayName } from "@/queries/user";

export async function saveDisplayNameAction(name: string) {
  await updateUserDisplayName(prisma, userId, name);
}
```

### ❌ Bad: query in a `src/lib/<domain>/*.ts` helper

```ts
// src/lib/auth/usernameChangeCooldown.ts
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma";

export async function assertUsernameChangeCooldownAllows(userId: string, now: Date) {
  const user = await prisma.user.findUnique({
    where: { id: userId },
    select: { usernameChangedAt: true },
  });
  // ...
}
```

### ✅ Good: `src/lib/<domain>/*.ts` helper delegates to a query module

```ts
// src/queries/user.ts
import type { PrismaClient } from "../../generated/prisma/client";

export async function findUsernameChangedAt(db: PrismaClient, userId: string) {
  return db.user.findUnique({
    where: { id: userId },
    select: { usernameChangedAt: true },
  });
}
```

```ts
// src/lib/auth/usernameChangeCooldown.ts
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma";
import { findUsernameChangedAt } from "@/queries/user";

export async function assertUsernameChangeCooldownAllows(userId: string, now: Date) {
  const row = await findUsernameChangedAt(prisma, userId);
  // ... pure cooldown logic on `row.usernameChangedAt` ...
}
```

## Transactions

For operations that must be atomic, expose a single query function that wraps the work in `db.$transaction`, and call it from the action. Do not open a `prisma.$transaction` block inside a page or action.

```ts
// ✅ Good: transaction lives in the data layer
// src/queries/user.ts
export async function applyEmailChange(db: PrismaClient, userId: string, newEmail: string, now: Date) {
  return db.$transaction(async (tx) => {
    await tx.user.update({ where: { id: userId }, data: { email: newEmail, emailVerified: false, unverifiedGraceStartsAt: now } });
    await tx.account.updateMany({ where: { userId, providerId: "credential" }, data: { accountId: newEmail } });
  });
}
```

## Typing

Use Prisma-generated types (`Prisma`, `PrismaClient`, model types) for parameters and return values. Avoid manually redefining types that already come from the schema.

## When you add a new query

1. Decide the target file in `src/queries/` (one file per model, or a domain file when the query spans models).
2. Name the function after its responsibility (`getUserById`, `listActiveProductTypeKeys`, `updateUserDisplayName`).
3. Export Prisma-derived input/return types next to the function.
4. Call it from the page/action, passing the `prisma` client when the function uses DI.
5. Never introduce `prisma.<model>.<method>(...)` outside of `src/queries/**` and tests — not in `src/app/**`, `src/components/**`, `src/hooks/**`, `src/context/**`, or `src/lib/**`.
